.TH CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER 3 "2026-08-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER \- declare buffer for ranged memory pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cmempool.h>

CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER(
    name,
    smallest_size_power_of_two,
    largest_size_power_of_two,
    number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a uint8_t array sized correctly for a ranged memory pool with
the specified parameters. The buffer can then be passed to
ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(). Automatically includes
every sub-pool's own per-element status bytes in the size calculation;
the buffer contains all sub-pools in contiguous memory.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name                                     Variable name for the buffer
smallest_size_power_of_two               Exponent for smallest pool size
largest_size_power_of_two                Exponent for largest pool size
number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two  Exponent for smallest pool count
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Buffer is allocated on stack or in static storage depending on context
Useful for embedded systems or avoiding heap allocation
Buffer is declared with the alignment every entry the pool hands out is
  guaranteed to meet, which is 16 bytes, so it can be handed
  directly to ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer() with no
  further alignment considerations; every individual sub-pool segment
  within the buffer stays correctly aligned as a consequence
Size is computed via CCOL_CALCULATE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER_SIZE
Rejected at compile time, via a _Static_assert, rather than silently
  producing a wrongly-sized array: parameters that would make the required
  buffer size overflow size_t; a
  number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two below
  largest_size_power_of_two - smallest_size_power_of_two; and a
  smallest_size_power_of_two below 4, since 16 bytes is the smallest
  element a ranged pool serves and a buffer declared for anything
  smaller could only be handed to a constructor that refuses it
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER(my_buffer, 4, 8, 10);
ccol_r_mempool *rmp = ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(
    my_buffer, sizeof(my_buffer), 4, 8, 10,
    ccol_fallback_disabled, false, NULL, NULL);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer (3)
